#!/bin/sh
# op8-mangoapp (armdeck): runs mangoapp-gs (MangoHud built for gamescope 3.16.29, see
# build-mangoapp-gs.sh) in the "steam" container for as long as the Steam session lives.
# steam-in-container.sh starts it in the background right before "exec steam", so its parent
# becomes the Steam process; when Steam exits, the loop stops.
# The container has ipc=host, so mangoapp sees gamescope's message queue on the host.
# Log: ~/op8-mangoapp.log
BIN=/home/gabriel/games/build/mangoapp-gs
LOG=/home/gabriel/op8-mangoapp.log
PIDF=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}/op8-mangoapp.pid

[ -x "$BIN" ] || exit 0

# one instance only: stop the loop and the mangoapp left over from an earlier session
if [ -f "$PIDF" ] && kill -0 "$(cat "$PIDF")" 2>/dev/null; then
	kill "$(cat "$PIDF")" 2>/dev/null
fi
pkill -x mangoapp-gs 2>/dev/null
echo $$ > "$PIDF"

STEAM_PID=$PPID
# gamescope and mangoapp pick the queue with ftok("mangoapp", 65), relative to the current
# directory; with no "mangoapp" file in either directory the key is the same (-1), so run from /
cd /
echo "$(date '+%F %T') started, Steam session = $STEAM_PID" >> "$LOG"
while kill -0 "$STEAM_PID" 2>/dev/null; do
	"$BIN" >> "$LOG" 2>&1
	echo "$(date '+%F %T') mangoapp-gs exited with $?" >> "$LOG"
	sleep 2
done
rm -f "$PIDF"
